WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Before writing validation code, define what valid data means.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.600
Go types describe structure, but they rarely describe the full contract.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:18.700
A string field does not say whether it is required, how long it may be, which characters are allowed, or whether it represents a username, tenant, file name, status, or email address.

4
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.600
An int does not say whether negative values are allowed or whether a value above a business limit should be rejected.

5
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.560
Go zero values make this design step especially important.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.100
A missing JSON number may decode to zero, and zero may also be a legitimate value.

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.440
A missing string may decode to an empty string, and an empty string may or may not be acceptable.

8
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:48.580
Pointer fields can help distinguish a value that was omitted from a value that was provided as zero, false, or empty.

9
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:55.240
The choice should be intentional because it affects both validation and API compatibility.

10
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:08.720
Required fields, optional fields, allowed values, numeric ranges, string lengths, nested structs, slices, maps, and cross-field rules all belong in the contract.

11
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.500
Go does not have first-class enums, so teams often use constants and custom types by convention.

12
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.640
That convention still needs validation when external input is converted into the custom type.

13
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.780
A status string that parses into a known value is different from any arbitrary string.

14
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.940
Clear contracts also include business rules.

15
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.760
A start time should come before an end time.

16
00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.340
A page size should fit service limits.

17
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.000
A list of IDs may need a maximum length and duplicate handling.

18
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.140
A discount code may only apply to certain plans.

19
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:53.640
When these rules are defined before processing input, the code can reject invalid requests early and pass well-understood values into persistence, authorization, file access, or workflow logic.
